1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
It is eleven at night, and Nora is
watching her clothes spin.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,000
The laundromat hums.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,000
A green backpack sits between her feet,
the same one she carried through college.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Her phone lies face up on the folding
table.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:15,000
No messages.

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Not one.

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,000
She checks it anyway.

8
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Three weeks ago, she moved to a city where
she knows nobody.

9
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Two weeks ago, the job that was supposed
to justify the move disappeared.

10
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Tonight, the loudest voice in her life is
a dryer.

11
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Most of us picture a certain kind of
person falling into a cult.

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Gullible.

13
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Troubled.

14
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Easy to fool.

15
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
But what if that picture is the first lie?

16
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,000
What if the only thing a cult truly needs
is a night exactly like this one?

17
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
In a few minutes, Nora will say yes to
something small.

18
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Over the next eleven months, she will pass
through seven stages, one after another.

19
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Each will feel gentle.

20
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Each will feel reasonable.

21
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,667
And somewhere along the way, a stranger
far outside her sight will begin pulling

22
00:00:58,667 --> 00:01:00,000
on a single loose thread.

23
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Psychiatrist Robert Jay Lifton laid out
the machinery in

24
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
after interviewing people who had lived
through thought reform in China.

25
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,333
Decades later, psychologist Margaret
Singer, working with Janja Lalich on a

26
00:01:12,333 --> 00:01:18,625
book called Cults in Our Midst, showed how
the same machinery turns up much closer to

27
00:01:18,625 --> 00:01:19,000
home.

28
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Neither of them found a door marked
danger.

29
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
They found a door marked welcome.

30
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Here is the idea this story is built on.

31
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
A cult does not seize a mind.

32
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,417
It builds a room, one comfortable wall at
a time, and waits for a person to walk

33
00:01:35,417 --> 00:01:37,000
into it on their own.

34
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Is that really so unusual?

35
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,708
Singer spent decades working with former
members, and she described recruits as

36
00:01:42,708 --> 00:01:46,625
people who were often lonely, between
attachments, and sometimes mildly

37
00:01:46,625 --> 00:01:47,000
depressed.

38
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,000
A Education Week report on her work added
something surprising.

39
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,208
A researcher quoted there said that people
who join cults are generally not the

40
00:01:58,208 --> 00:02:00,000
children of troubled families.

41
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
They were people in transition.

42
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:02,000
People between things.

43
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
People like Nora.

44
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The group she is about to meet calls
itself the Harbor.

45
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,750
Its leader, Julian Marrow, is invented for
this story, but every move he makes comes

46
00:02:11,750 --> 00:02:13,000
straight from the research.

47
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:20,042
And by the time the walls become visible,
the person inside has usually helped to

48
00:02:20,042 --> 00:02:21,000
build them.

49
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
So how does someone get from a laundromat
to a locked kitchen?

50
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
One stage at a time.

51
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Stage one is the crack.

52
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:31,583
A man at the next machine is folding
towels very slowly, the way people do when

53
00:02:31,583 --> 00:02:34,000
they want to be asked about it.

54
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
His name is Daniel.

55
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:38,000
He volunteers at a community dinner on
Thursdays.

56
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Free food.

57
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,000
About twenty people.

58
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Nobody pushes anything.

59
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Nora hears herself say she might come.

60
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,042
Researchers describe recruiters working
where people are in motion, near campuses,

61
00:02:50,042 --> 00:02:56,000
bus depots, and new neighborhoods, because
a person in motion has no one

62
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
nearby who knows what normal looks like
for them.

63
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,000
And notice what has not happened.

64
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Nobody has mentioned a belief.

65
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Nobody has mentioned a leader.

66
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,792
Singer listed six conditions that make
this kind of influence possible, and the

67
00:03:09,792 --> 00:03:12,000
first sounds almost insultingly simple.

68
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,000
The person must not know there is any
plan.

69
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Nora does not know there is one.

70
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Maybe there is not, yet.

71
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Maybe Daniel is just lonely too.

72
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Which raises the question that haunts
every story like this one.

73
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,583
How often is the first friendly face a
recruiter, and how often is it simply a

74
00:03:30,583 --> 00:03:32,000
friend who was recruited himself?

75
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Stage two is the warmth.

76
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
On Thursday, the room smells like garlic
and wet coats.

77
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Strangers learn her name in the first five
minutes and remember it.

78
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
A woman asks about her green backpack.

79
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
A man laughs at something she says, a real
laugh, and it goes straight through her.

80
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,000
For three weeks nobody has laughed at
anything she said.

81
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
By Sunday, she has been hugged eleven
times.

82
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:55,000
She counts.

83
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
She has also been given a nickname, a seat
at the table, and a standing invitation.

84
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,333
Steven Hassan, who spent time in the
Unification Church in the 1970s before

85
00:04:04,333 --> 00:04:08,333
becoming a cult researcher and counselor,
lists emotional extremes among the main

86
00:04:08,333 --> 00:04:13,000
tools of control, and he places the early
flood of affection, often called love

87
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:14,000
bombing, among them.

88
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
The warmth is not fake, exactly.

89
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
That is what makes it powerful.

90
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
So what is it for?

91
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
The Harbor has a quiet rule.

92
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Warmth rises when Nora attends, and cools,
just slightly, when she does not.

93
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Nobody says it out loud.

94
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Nobody has to.

95
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,542
That same week, across town, a local
reporter named Ines Calloway receives an

96
00:04:32,542 --> 00:04:33,000
email from a stranger.

97
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
It is signed with a single letter.

98
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:36,000
M.

99
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
The message is short.

100
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Look into the Harbor.

101
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,000
Ines files it under later.

102
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Stage three is the story.

103
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Julian Marrow does not appear until week
five.

104
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
He is soft-spoken, and he never raises his
voice, which makes the room lean in.

105
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,875
He explains that the world is loud on
purpose, a machine built to distract, and

106
00:04:54,875 --> 00:04:57,000
that the Harbor is where people learn to
hear the quiet.

107
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
It sounds beautiful.

108
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Parts of it are.

109
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Then the vocabulary arrives.

110
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Outsiders are the Drift.

111
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Doubt is static.

112
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Leaving is slipping.

113
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,167
When Nora asks a question, someone smiles
and says one phrase, that's just static,

114
00:05:18,167 --> 00:05:20,000
and the question dissolves like sugar in
tea.

115
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Lifton called this loading the language.

116
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:31,583
He also described what he called sacred
science, the idea that a group's teaching

117
00:05:31,583 --> 00:05:33,000
is beyond question.

118
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Notice how pleasant it feels.

119
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,000
Doubt used to be a problem.

120
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Now it has a label, and the label makes it
somebody else's fault.

121
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Is that peace, or is it a locked drawer?

122
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Stage four is the small yes.

123
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Nothing in the Harbor asks for everything
at once.

124
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,000
It asks for a Tuesday evening.

125
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Then six a.m.

126
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
meditation on Wednesdays.

127
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,833
Then a weekend retreat, forty miles away,
which means she will need to skip her temp

128
00:05:59,833 --> 00:06:00,000
shifts.

129
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Each yes is small enough to be reasonable.

130
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Together, they do something larger.

131
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,750
Singer's second condition is control of a
person's time and surroundings, and Hassan

132
00:06:13,750 --> 00:06:15,000
calls the wider pattern behavior control.

133
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Nobody takes Nora's time.

134
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
She hands it over in pieces.

135
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
At the retreat, there is a ritual of
confession.

136
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,833
Each member says aloud what they are
ashamed of, to a partner who writes it

137
00:06:24,833 --> 00:06:25,000
down.

138
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Lifton wrote that in such environments
confession has no confidentiality.

139
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
What you reveal becomes part of the file.

140
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Nora tells her partner about her father.

141
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
About the year she stopped speaking to her
sister.

142
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,000
It feels like relief.

143
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,000
It is also, quietly, an archive.

144
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
The following week, a business card is
taped to the Harbor's front door.

145
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Ines Calloway, reporter.

146
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Seeking former members.

147
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Julian reads it aloud at dinner and
smiles.

148
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
Reporters, he says, are the loudest static
of all.

149
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:00,000
Everyone laughs with him.

150
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:00,000
Nora laughs too.

151
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,000
Nobody asks what Ines wanted to ask.

152
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Stage five is the walls.

153
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
By month five, Nora lives in the Harbor's
house.

154
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
The rent is lower.

155
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:12,000
The people are kinder.

156
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
Her old lease ended anyway.

157
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
The walls are not made of stone.

158
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
They are made of schedules.

159
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Phones go into a basket during quiet
hours, and the quiet hours keep growing.

160
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:27,667
Her sister Leah calls, and the call goes
to voicemail, and then Nora stops

161
00:07:27,667 --> 00:07:32,000
listening to the voicemails, because
Julian once said that people who love you

162
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
but do not understand you can feel like
weather.

163
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:38,000
Old friends become the Drift.

164
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,000
Any book outside the reading list feels,
somehow, noisy.

165
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,417
Lifton called this milieu control, the
control of communication, which can grow

166
00:07:43,417 --> 00:07:45,000
until it reaches inside a person's own
thoughts.

167
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:49,000
Hassan calls the related tool information
control.

168
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
And notice, there are no guards here.

169
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
Nobody has ever stopped Nora from leaving.

170
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
The door is unlocked.

171
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:02,000
The trouble is that there is no one left
outside to leave toward.

172
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,250
Meanwhile, in Ines Calloway's inbox, a
second email arrives from the woman who

173
00:08:05,250 --> 00:08:06,000
signs herself M.

174
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Her name is Mara.

175
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:12,000
She left the Harbor two years ago.

176
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:13,000
This time, Ines writes back.

177
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Stage six is the weight.

178
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:19,000
Julian announces the Crossing.

179
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
A date in the spring when the Drift will
fall away and the Harbor will rise.

180
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:24,000
Members begin to give more.

181
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:25,000
Savings.

182
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:25,000
A car.

183
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:35,000
A sublet.

184
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,208
It is because she has already given so
much that stopping now would mean

185
00:08:39,208 --> 00:08:41,000
admitting it was all for nothing.

186
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:42,000
In

187
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:46,833
a woman in the American Midwest named
Dorothy Martin told a small group of

188
00:08:46,833 --> 00:08:51,000
followers that a great flood would arrive
before dawn on December twenty-first.

189
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,583
Social psychologist Leon Festinger and his
colleagues secretly joined the group to

190
00:08:54,583 --> 00:08:57,000
watch what would happen when it did not.

191
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
They published their account in in a book
called When Prophecy Fails.

192
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:04,000
The flood never came.

193
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,125
And according to the researchers, the
believers who had invested the most were

194
00:09:08,125 --> 00:09:10,000
the least likely to walk away.

195
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
Many tried to spread the message harder.

196
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:16,458
Later scholars have criticized the study,
since it followed a tiny group, and the

197
00:09:16,458 --> 00:09:19,000
observers may have shaped what they were
watching.

198
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,000
But the pattern it described has stayed
with us.

199
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
The more you pay, the harder it is to say
the price was too high.

200
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,000
The date of the Crossing arrives.

201
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,000
It passes.

202
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Nothing falls.

203
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:32,000
Nothing rises.

204
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Julian stands before them and says the
date was a test, and that they passed it.

205
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
And Nora, to her own surprise, cries with
relief.

206
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:44,000
That week, everyone is asked to confess
again, this time about their doubts.

207
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Nora's confession from the retreat is read
back to her, gently, as a reminder.

208
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
The Harbor knows everything about her.

209
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
The Harbor loves her anyway.

210
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Or is that the other way around?

211
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
Stage seven is the crack, turned inside
out.

212
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:03,708
You might be thinking that this is only a
story about one small group in one small

213
00:10:03,708 --> 00:10:04,000
city.

214
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
But the pattern is older, and it has cost
far more.

215
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:08,000
In June of

216
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:12,833
a woman named Deborah Layton, who had
recently escaped a group called Peoples

217
00:10:12,833 --> 00:10:17,292
Temple, signed a sworn affidavit warning
the United States government that American

218
00:10:17,292 --> 00:10:21,000
lives were threatened in a settlement
called Jonestown, in Guyana.

219
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
Many people did not believe her.

220
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Some relatives did.

221
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,250
A California congressman named Leo Ryan
traveled there that November, and some

222
00:10:30,250 --> 00:10:32,000
residents left with him.

223
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Ryan was killed at an airstrip.

224
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:37,000
More than nine hundred people died in
Jonestown that day.

225
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
The affidavit was not wrong.

226
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
It was only early.

227
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
Mara has read that affidavit.

228
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:45,000
It is why she kept writing.

229
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:46,000
One sworn page can be a door.

230
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,000
In this story, the door opens in the
middle of a Tuesday.

231
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
Ines Calloway's article runs.

232
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:56,000
County investigators arrive with papers
about the Harbor's money.

233
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,000
The house fills with strangers carrying
boxes.

234
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:04,000
By morning, Julian Marrow is gone, and a
dozen people stand in the kitchen, holding

235
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,000
mugs, with no one left to tell them what

236
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:08,000
the silence means.

237
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Nora walks out into the yard.

238
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:11,000
A car is idling by the curb.

239
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:16,750
And on her phone, which someone has
finally returned to her, there is a

240
00:11:16,750 --> 00:11:18,000
voicemail from Leah.

241
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:22,000
It is thirty seconds long.

242
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
Your room is still yellow, her sister
says.

243
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:24,000
I am outside.

244
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
I will not make you come.

245
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
I just want to hand you your coat.

246
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:29,000
Nora stands very still.

247
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:34,125
Lifton observed that when the control over
a person's environment is lifted,

248
00:11:34,125 --> 00:11:38,000
something of the earlier self begins to
reassert itself.

249
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:42,000
It does not happen all at once.

250
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
It happens like feeling returning to a
sleeping arm, which hurts before it helps.

251
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:51,000
So what does all of this actually mean?

252
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,000
The crack.

253
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:55,000
The warmth.

254
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:55,000
The story.

255
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:56,000
The small yes.

256
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:57,000
The walls.

257
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:58,000
The weight.

258
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
None of them required a stupid person.

259
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,458
Each required a person who was lonely on a
Tuesday night, and a group that happened

260
00:12:05,458 --> 00:12:06,000
to notice.

261
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
It is worth being honest about the limits.

262
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,958
Researchers do not agree on how to name
every piece of this, and some have

263
00:12:11,958 --> 00:12:13,000
criticized models like Hassan's.

264
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:20,000
Lifton himself warned that these traits
exist in degrees, and can be found, in

265
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
small amounts, in many ordinary
institutions.

266
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:24,000
The list is a lens.

267
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
It is not a verdict.

268
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
But the lens shows something true.

269
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
Most of us will never meet a Julian
Marrow.

270
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
We will meet a Daniel.

271
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
A warm hand.

272
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:35,000
A seat at a table.

273
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:43,875
And the real question is whether, on our
own worst Tuesday, we would hear the

274
00:12:43,875 --> 00:12:48,000
difference between being welcomed and
being collected.

275
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Leah does not say anything.

276
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
She just turns up the heat.

277
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:54,000
At the first red light, Nora's phone
buzzes again.

278
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
It is Daniel.

279
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
We miss you, the message says.

280
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
Static can't hurt you.

281
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:01,000
Come home.

282
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:02,000
She reads it twice.

283
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:03,000
She does not answer.

284
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
She does not delete it either.

285
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
Somewhere tonight, a laundromat is
humming.

286
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:13,000
A phone is lying face up on a folding
table, waiting for a message.

287
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:18,292
And a stranger is folding towels very
slowly, wondering who will ask about them

288
00:13:18,292 --> 00:13:18,667
first.
